lugin).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Given the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins source, when the workspace provider initialization path is traced, then every external dependency it requires (workspace store, tracking store, registry store, other) is enumerated, measured by a documented dependency inventory.\n- Given the findings, a clear coupled/not-coupled determination is recorded with the code-level evidence, measured by a written conclusion referencing the specific initialization path.\n- If coupled, the refactor scope (independent initialization path for workspace resolution) is described with the affected plugin modules; if not coupled, the verification evidence (workspace resolution succeeds without tracking store initialization) is documented.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR \"[P1] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\" — this investigation resolves the unknown that determines that epic's scope.\n\n---\n\n**E002** (Investigation, MLflow, P0)\n\nTitle: Investigate artifact path resolvability for workspace validation in artifacts-only mode\n\nDescription: Resolve whether artifact request paths handled by the MLflow artifacts-only server carry sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership, per the strategy assumption \"needs validation\" and the risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\". The strategy's design resolves each artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/) and looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store. This investigation validates that assumption for the scoped configuration (S3 artifact store, standard RHOAI layout) and documents limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\nScope:\n- Trace how the artifacts-only proxy parses and serves artifact request paths (/api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/* endpoints) in the MLflow server source.\n- Validate that, for the S3 artifact store with the standard {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/ layout, each artifact request path resolves to a parent experiment ID.\n- Determine how run-ID-only paths (if any) resolve to experiment workspace membership.\n- Document artifact root configurations where resolution is not possible and the required behavior for them (deny, per fail-closed design).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Given artifact request paths from the artifacts-only API surface, when parsed per the S3 standard layout, then each path form's resolvability to an experiment (and thereby workspace) is classified, measured by a documented path-form inventory with resolvable/unresolvable determination.\n- Given unresolvable path forms, the required handling (request denied — fail-closed) is specified for the implementation, measured by a written recommendation.\n- Limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n\nHLR: P0-2, P0-3 (validation and fail-closed design depend on this resolution).\n\n---\n\n**E003** (Investigation, MLflow Operator, P1)\n\nTitle: Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology\n\nDescription: Resolve how the main MLflow tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy's open question (owner: MLflow Team) asks how the operator should configure this routing; the answer affects HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. Two bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing suffices — HTTPRoute path-based rules route artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance with no tracking-server change; (B) the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations.\n\nScope:\n- Analyze the artifact API request paths served by the main tracking deployment vs the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Determine whether Gateway API HTTPRoute path matching (consistent with the operator's existing conditional CRD discovery pattern) can route artifact traffic without tracking-server changes.\n- Determine what tracking-server-side change (if any) is needed if gateway-level routing is insufficient.\n- Produce a decision record selecting the routing mechanism, with the HTTPRoute design implications.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- A bounded outcome is recorded: gateway-level routing sufficient, or tracking-server delegation required, measured by a written decision with supporting analysis.\n- The selected mechanism's implications for HTTPRoute design and main tracking deployment configuration are documented.\n\nHLR: P1-6 (main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance).\n\nConditional branches: outcome A → branch A epic (gateway-level routing, operator component); outcome B → branch B epic (tracking-server delegation, MLflow component). Hmm — careful: \"No cross-references to sibling epics... do not reference other epics by draft ID\". For the INVESTIGATION epic E003, describing branch outcomes is fine as long as I don't name \"E009\" etc. I'll describe outcomes without epic IDs. Actually for the summary I can use IDs; for epic bodies, no. I'll describe branches in plain terms.\n\n---\n\n**E004** (Implementation, MLflow, P0)\n\nTitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\nDescription: Make --artifacts-only composable with --enable-workspaces in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit --workspace-store-uri, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled. Each artifact request is resolved to its parent experiment, the experiment's workspace assignment is looked up via the workspace store, and authorization is delegated to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403. The mode is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied (HTTP 503 per strategy AC). Single-tenant deployments and configurations without --enable-workspaces retain current behavior. Includes audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs (P2 HLR, bundled here because it shares the denial-handling path). No new server flag is introduced (Staff Engineer direction).\n\nScope:\n- Server initialization: allow --artifacts-only + --enable-workspaces combination; require --workspace-store-uri when both set (fail to start with clear error if missing, per dependency note); initialize only the workspace provider; keep tracking/model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact endpoints disabled.\n- Artifact request handling: resolve artifact path to parent experiment; look up workspace assignment via workspace store; delegate authorization to existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny failing requests with HTTP 403.\n- Fail-closed behavior: deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 per strategy AC) when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable.\n- Backwards compatibility: --artifacts-only without --enable-workspaces preserves current behavior; omitting either flag preserves current behavior; no existing CLI arguments or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving are logged through server logs.\n- Performance: measure per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions if overhead exceeds the acceptable threshold determined by PM/Engineering (consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path).\n- Scope initial implementation to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration); document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- AC-1: Given the artifacts-only instance started with --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served, measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n- AC-2: Given workspace-aware serving enabled and a user member of workspace A but not B, when the user requests an artifact of an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 403 with error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- AC-3: Given --artifacts-only without --enable-workspaces, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then it is served without workspace validation, measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership.\n- AC-4: Given workspace-aware mode active and the workspace store unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable.\n- AC-5: Given --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces without --workspace-store-uri, the server fails to start with a clear error (per Dependencies table).\n- AC-6: Given workspace-aware mode, non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled and tracking/model registry stores remain uninitialized, measured by server startup behavior/endpoint availability.\n- AC-7: Workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit log entries through server logs.\n- AC-8: Per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and documented; short-TTL caching is implemented if the measured overhead exceeds the threshold agreed with PM/Engineering.\n- Rollback/backwards-compat: behavior activates only when both flags are set (feature is flag-gated by design); no API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys removed or changed in meaning.\n\nHLR Traceability: P0-1, P0-2, P0-3, P0-4, P2-8 (audit logging). \n\nRisks section: artifact path resolution complexity (scoped to S3); performance impact (caching mitigation); fail modes and bypass vectors to be confirmed by the fail-mode and security analyses (gating).\n\nWait — E004 depends on E007 and E008 (investigations). Should its body mention that? \"Dependencies\" in plain terms: \"This epic is gated by the investigations resolving artifact path resolvability, failure modes, and security bypass vectors, and by the plugin workspace-resolution capability for the artifacts-only context.\" Plain terms, no IDs. OK.\n\n---\n\n**E005** (Implementation, plugin, P0)\n\nTitle: Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n\nDescription: Verify and extend the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin so workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The plugin (registered through the mlflow.workspace_provider entry point group, loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry) already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking server's non-artifact operations. If the initialization-coupling investigation finds workspace resolution coupled to the full tracking server initialization path, factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and --app-name kubernetes-auth setup are unchanged.\n\nScope:\n- Verify plugin workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace mapping, workspace store access) in a server context with tracking/model registry stores uninitialized.\n- If coupled: refactor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n- Ensure workspace resolution can be driven from artifact request paths (delegated from the server's artifact request handling).\n- Preserve existing behavior for the full tracking server context (no regression to non-artifact operations).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Given an MLflow server in artifacts-only mode with workspace flags set, the plugin's workspace resolution initializes and resolves experiment-to-workspace