ntime CRD discovery.\n\nWait — should \"main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic\" be in E006 or in the branch epics? I put routing implementation in branch epics. E006 scope: deploy dedicated instance. But the strategy's affected components says operator: \"Deploy dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment with Service and HTTPRoute; configure artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment\". Hmm. The routing config is conditional on E003's outcome. I've modeled the routing work as branch epics. But E006 could include \"whatever main-deployment configuration changes are needed independent of routing mechanism\". Let me keep routing in the branches and note in E006 that routing configuration is handled per the selected mechanism. Hmm, but then in branch A (gateway routing), the routing work is operator-side — BRANCH-A-E009 covers it. In branch B, server-side + operator config — BRANCH-B-E009. OK. E006 scope covers the dedicated instance deployment + validation; main-tracking routing config lands in the branch epic. I'll note this.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- AC-5 (strategy): Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the operator reconciles, then a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute are created separate from the main tracking Deployment, measured by presence of distinct Deployment resources with correct flag combinations.\n- AC-6 (strategy): the artifacts-only Deployment runs with --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts, measured by inspecting the container command in the rendered Deployment spec.\n- Operator rejects invalid configurations (artifacts-only instance without workspace flags), measured by validation error on such a CR.\n- HTTPRoute creation follows conditional CRD discovery (no hard Gateway API dependency), measured by behavior on clusters without Gateway API CRDs.\n- The deployed artifacts-only image supports the combined flags (configuration reference — verified against the MLflow server capability at execution time, not a build dependency).\n\nHLR: P1-5; supports P1-6.\n\n---\n\n**E007** (Investigation, MLflow, P0) — fail-mode analysis\n\nTitle: Fail-mode analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving\n\nDescription: Enumerate and classify the failure modes of workspace-aware artifact serving, confirming fail-closed coverage. The strategy specifies fail-closed behavior for an unreachable workspace store, but the full failure-mode surface of the split topology (unresolvable artifact paths, plugin initialization failures, workspace store errors, partial topology misconfiguration where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags, timeouts) must be enumerated with a defined required behavior for each, and any modes requiring remediation beyond the specified design identified.\n\nScope:\n- Enumerate failure modes across the workspace-aware artifact serving path: workspace store unreachable/errors, artifact paths that do not resolve to an experiment, plugin initialization failure, malformed requests, partial split-topology misconfiguration.\n- For each mode, define required behavior (deny with appropriate status, log) consistent with the fail-closed design.\n- Identify any modes where the specified design does not fail closed and would require additional remediation work.\n- Deliverable: failure-mode matrix with required behaviors and remediation list.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Every enumerated failure mode has a defined required behavior (deny/serve) and status, measured by a complete failure-mode matrix.\n- Any mode where the current design would not fail closed is identified with proposed remediation, measured by a remediation list (empty if none).\n\nHLR: P0-3 (fail-closed); P0-2.\n\n---\n\n**E008** (Investigation, MLflow, P0) — security bypass analysis\n\nTitle: Security bypass analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving in the split-server topology\n\nDescription: Analyze bypass vectors for workspace validation in the split-server topology. The strategy's risk \"Split-deployment operational complexity\" notes that misconfiguration could result in artifact requests bypassing workspace validation. This investigation identifies and evaluates bypass vectors — crafted artifact paths that escape experiment scoping, direct access to the artifacts-only instance bypassing the intended routing, operator misconfiguration, interactions with the unchanged kube-rbac-proxy authentication layer — and maps mitigations to the implementation work.\n\nScope:\n- Threat analysis of the workspace validation path in the artifacts-only server (path manipulation, encoding, paths resolving to no experiment).\n- Network/routing bypass vectors in the split topology (direct instance access, HTTPRoute misrouting).\n- Operator misconfiguration vectors (artifacts-only instance deployed without workspace flags).\n- Map each vector to a mitigation in the server implementation, plugin, or operator validation; identify unmitigated vectors requiring additional work.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Bypass vectors are enumerated with evaluated exploitability, measured by a documented threat analysis.\n- Each vector is mapped to a mitigation (or confirmed blocked by design), measured by a vector→mitigation matrix.\n- Unmitigated vectors are identified with recommended remediation scope.\n\nHLR: P0-2, P0-3; NFR Security.\n\n---\n\n**BRANCH-A-E009** (Implementation, operator, P1, branch A)\n\nTitle: Gateway-level artifact traffic routing to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\nDescription: Conditional on the routing investigation selecting gateway-level routing: configure Gateway API HTTPRoute path-based routing so artifact API traffic entering through the main tracking deployment's route is routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, with no tracking-server change. \n\nScope:\n- HTTPRoute path rules for artifact API paths routing to the dedicated artifacts-only Service.\n- Any main-route/gateway configuration needed to split artifact traffic from tracking traffic.\n- End-to-end validation that artifact requests routed through the main entry point reach the dedicated instance and are subject to workspace validation (coordinated with the workspace-aware artifacts-only server capability).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Given a user requesting an artifact through the main tracking entry point, the request is served by the dedicated artifacts-only instance with workspace validation applied, measured by HTTP 200/403/503 responses consistent with workspace-aware serving.\n- Given non-artifact API requests, they continue to be served by the main tracking deployment, measured by tracking/model registry operations functioning unchanged.\n- Routing configuration follows the operator's conditional Gateway API CRD discovery pattern.\n\nHLR: P1-6 (this branch's realization).\n\ngate_failure_impact: action remove, fallback: \"If the investigation finds gateway-level routing insufficient, this epic is removed and the tracking-server delegation branch applies instead.\"\n\n---\n\n**BRANCH-B-E009** (Implementation, MLflow, P1, branch B)\n\nTitle: Tracking-server artifact delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\nDescription: Conditional on the routing investigation finding that the tracking server needs a configuration change: implement the main tracking server's delegation of artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the mechanism identified by the investigation (configuration and/or server-side change).\n\nScope:\n- Main tracking server configuration/change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance (mechanism per investigation outcome).\n- Preserve non-artifact operations (tracking, model registry, prompt registry) on the main tracking server.\n- End-to-end validation that delegated artifact requests are subject to workspace validation at the dedicated instance.\n\nAcceptance C