{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f86d5355-971a-47ee-8e05-e11ef2bd974d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4cf0ed8ab8c71c3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-lpattpc9/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009\n\n**Strategy**: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n**Status**: Full decomposition (not triaged below-threshold or docs-only)\n\n## Triage Rationale\n\nThe strategy affects three active components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins), is estimated M-L (4-7 sprints), and spans architecturally distinct work streams (Python server flag composition, plugin workspace resolution, Go operator split-deployment topology with Helm chart templates). Neither Step 0 check applies.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace-resolution initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path resolvability for workspace validation | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate split-topology artifact traffic routing mechanism | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Fail-mode analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | Security bypass analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n\n### Conditional branch epics (attached to E003, not standalone Jira issues)\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Branch |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|--------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009 | Gateway-level artifact traffic routing to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | A |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009 | Tracking-server artifact delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | B |\n\n## Dependency DAG (main plan)\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n    E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n    E007[RHAISTRAT-8009-E007] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n    E008[RHAISTRAT-8009-E008] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003] --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006]\n    E008[RHAISTRAT-8009-E008] --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006]\n```\n\n### Conditional branches from E003\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003] --> BA[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009]\n    E003 --> BB[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009]\n    E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006] --> BA\n    E006 --> BB\n```\n\nOnly one branch is realized, depending on the E003 outcome. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Open question \"Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" determines whether the plugin work is verification-only or requires refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — scope/approach gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E001 → E005 (existence) | Rule 3 | The unknown is strategy-flagged (open question, risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\", assumption \"workspace store accessible without initializing tracking stores\") and its answer changes what the plugin epic does. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 | Assumption \"artifact request paths include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged needs-validation; if paths do not resolve, the server's workspace-validation approach rewrites (`rewrite` gate). |\n| E005 → E004 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The MLflow server's artifacts-only workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution (loaded via `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / WorkspaceStoreRegistry). If the plugin's initialization path is refactored, the server-side wiring consumes that change. |\n| E007 → E004 | Rule 19 (safety-critical: fail-mode Investigation) | Workspace-aware artifact serving is a multi-tenant access-control feature (tenant isolation, fail-closed authorization). The fail-mode analysis enumerates failure modes and confirms fail-closed coverage; may reveal modes requiring additional remediation (`add_remediation` gate). |\n| E008 → E004 | Rule 19 (safety-critical: security Investigation) | Bypass-vector analysis (crafted paths, direct instance access) gates the main server implementation; may reveal remediation work (`add_remediation` gate). |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing?\" — answer \"affects HTTPRoute design\", so the operator topology epic is gated (`rewrite`). |\n| E008 → E006 | Rule 19 | Risk \"Split-deployment operational complexity\" states misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation; the security analysis covers operator misconfiguration vectors and gates the topology implementation (`add_remediation` gate). |\n| E003 → branch epics | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes → conditional branches) | Two bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing suffices; (B) tracking-server delegation required. Each outcome selects a different branch epic (`remove` gate on the non-selected branch). |\n| E006 → branch epics | Rule 12/13 | Both routing branches configure traffic toward the dedicated artifacts-only instance; they consume the deployed instance produced by the operator topology epic. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E007 ∥ E008 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Independent investigations resolving distinct unknowns (plugin init coupling, path resolvability, routing mechanism, failure modes, bypass vectors); no shared deliverable — not combined. |\n| E004 ∥ E006 (no edge) | Rule 14 + Rule 13 clarification | Different repos (mlflow vs mlflow-operator); the operator renders Helm templates referencing server flags — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. Verified by AC (\"deployed image supports the combined flags\"), never a DAG edge. |\n| Branch epics ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rules 14/15 | Routing configuration does not build against the server's workspace-validation code (different repos / different areas in the same repo); end-to-end routing validation is an AC gate, not a DAG dependency. |\n| No docs-authoring epic | Step 5 rule 11 boundary | Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes) and no substantive content authoring is in scope; incidental doc touch-ups (documenting limitations for non-standard artifact roots) stay inside the originating epics as scope/AC. |\n| No onboarding chain | Rule 20 N/A | MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files and existing Konflux builds; mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is an existing internal component (see Health Warnings). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 |\n| Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004, E005 (enables), E002 (validates approach), E008 (validates no bypass) |\n| Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004, E007 (validates failure-mode coverage) |\n| Single-tenant / no-workspaces deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 |\n| Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) | P1 | E006 |\n| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance | P1 | E003 (selects mechanism), RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009 or RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional) |\n| Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context | P1 | E001 (resolves coupling unknown), E005 |\n| Audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E004 (bundled — shares the denial-handling code path with P0 workspace validation; not independently deferrable) |\n\nAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. P2 audit logging is bundled into E004 because it shares the 403-denial implementation surface with the P0 workspace-validation work (tightly coupled — splitting would duplicate the denial path).\n\n## Priority Notes\n\n- E005 and E001 carry P0 despite their directly-mapped HLR being P1: priority inheritance — the plugin's artifacts-only workspace resolution transitively enables the P0 workspace-validation HLR via E004.\n- E007 and E008 are P0: they block E004, which implements all P0 HLRs.\n- E003 and E006 are P1: they gate only P1 HLRs (operator topology and routing).\n\n## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n\nThe Staff Engineer Input section is non-empty and takes precedence; the AI-generated technical approach was corrected accordingly:\n\n1. **No new flag** — the proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed. All epic scope uses the combined-flags approach (`--artifacts-only` + `--enable-workspaces` + required `--workspace-store-uri`). No epic introduces a new server flag.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — E006 deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance (separate Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) and the routing work is decomposed as conditional epics on the routing-mechanism investigation; no epic treats this as a single CR field toggle.\n3. **SSAR detail over-specified** — epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview/pseudo-resource prescriptions in any epic scope). The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are explicitly unchanged.\n\n## Component Name Mappings\n\nCanonical component names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n\n- **MLflow Operator** → `MLflow` (no operator-specific canonical component exists; MLflow is the closest parent-level component). Applied to E003, E006, RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009.\n- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** → `MLflow` (closest parent-level component; the plugin ships inside the MLflow deployment per mlflow.md architecture context). Applied to E001, E005.\n- **MLflow** → `MLflow` (exact match). Applied to E002, E004, E007, E008, RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009.\n\n## Investigation Summary\n\n| Epic | Unknown resolved | Why it changes downstream structure/scope | Downstream gate |\n|------|------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------|\n| E001 | Plugin workspace-resolution initialization coupling | Determines verify-only vs refactor of the plugin | E005 (rewrite) |\n| E002 | Artifact path → experiment/workspace resolvability | Determines the server's workspace-validation approach | E004 (rewrite) |\n| E003 | Routing mechanism (gateway-level vs tracking-server delegation) | Determines which routing implementation exists and HTTPRoute design | E006 (rewrite); branch A/B epics (remove) |\n| E007 | Failure-mode surface of workspace-aware serving | May reveal modes requiring remediation beyond specified fail-closed design | E004 (add_remediation) |\n| E008 | Validation bypass vectors in split topology | May reveal unmitigated vectors requiring additional work in server/operator | E004, E006 (add_remediation) |\n\nOpen questions NOT promoted to Investigations (with handling):\n- **Latency overhead / caching at launch** (PM/Engineering) — does not change which epics exist; captured as measurement + conditional short-TTL caching AC on E004. See Health Warnings.\n- **Audit telemetry channel** (PM/Security) — HLR already specifies server logs; implemented as specified in E004, with scope-growth risk flagged in Health Warnings.\n- Assumption \"flags composable without breaking init sequence\" — the work itself (E004); failure = fix within the epic.\n- Assumption \"Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes\" — the work itself (E006).\n- Assumption \"single-tenant deployments do not set --enable-workspaces\" — backwards-compatibility AC on E004 (strategy HLR P0-4 requires current behavior preserved regardless).\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nE003 (routing mechanism investigation) has two bounded outcomes that change downstream structure:\n\n- **Branch A — gateway-level routing suffices**: HTTPRoute path-based rules route artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance; no tracking-server change. Realized by `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009` (MLflow Operator work).\n- **Branch B — tracking-server delegation required**: the main tracking server needs a configuration/code change to delegate artifact operations. Realized by `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009` (MLflow server work).\n\nBoth branch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8009-E003` (also in `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Priority observation (non-blocking)**: the P0 HLRs (server-side workspace-aware serving) only deliver user-facing value in multi-tenant deployments once the P1 operator topology (E006) and routing land. The P1 work sits on the value-realization path of the P0 scope — consider whether the operator epic should be pulled forward in planning.\n2. **Performance NFR has no latency target**: the strategy's Performance NFR defers to an open PM/Engineering question. Caching-at-launch vs deferred is undetermined; handled as a measurement + conditional-caching AC on E004. May spawn follow-up work.\n3. **Audit channel scope risk (P2)**: HLR-8 specifies server logs, but open question 4 (PM/Security) may expand the channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log). Implemented per HLR; scope growth would be a follow-up.\n4. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins not in architecture context**: no architecture context file exists for it, but the strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\" and mlflow.md documents it shipping in the MLflow image (v1.5.0, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`). Treated as an existing internal component — no repo-onboarding/konflux-onboarding chain generated. Flagged for human verification that the team's plugin repo is accessible for modification.\n5. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" — outstanding human gates at the strategy level that may affect epic sequencing.\n6. **Scope note (S3-only path resolution)**: per the strategy's risk mitigation, the initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store with the standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout; limitations for non-standard artifact roots must be documented (AC on E004).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API conditional CRD discovery, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar); no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is required. The P0 (server capability) → P1 (operator topology) ordering reflects HLR priorities, not dependency tiering.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":68505,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88698f90