{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-688ea9ec-b9a6-44f3-901c-5cc5cd65e706","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-862aefcfbbaa95a7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-lpattpc9/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E008.md","content":"# Security bypass analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nAnalyze bypass vectors for workspace validation in the split-server topology. The strategy's \"Split-deployment operational complexity\" risk notes that a misconfigured split topology could result in artifact requests bypassing workspace validation, silently degrading tenant isolation. This investigation identifies and evaluates bypass vectors — crafted artifact paths that escape experiment scoping, direct access to the artifacts-only instance bypassing the intended routing, operator misconfiguration, and interactions with the unchanged kube-rbac-proxy authentication layer — and maps mitigations to the implementation work. Unmitigated vectors require additional remediation, so this analysis gates the server and operator implementations. This investigation exists because the strategy is a safety-critical, multi-tenant access-control change (workspace/tenant isolation).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Threat analysis of the workspace validation path in the artifacts-only server: path manipulation and encoding tricks, paths that resolve to no experiment or to the wrong experiment, requests with missing or invalid workspace context.\n- Network and routing bypass vectors in the split topology: direct access to the artifacts-only instance or its Service bypassing the intended entry point, HTTPRoute misrouting.\n- Operator misconfiguration vectors: an artifacts-only instance deployed without workspace flags, workspace configuration not propagated.\n- Confirm that the unchanged authentication layer (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) behaves identically for the artifacts-only instance and cannot be used to skip authorization.\n- Map each vector to a mitigation in the server implementation, the plugin, or operator validation; identify unmitigated vectors requiring additional work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Bypass vectors are enumerated with evaluated exploitability, measured by a documented threat analysis.\n- Each vector is mapped to a mitigation or confirmed blocked by design, measured by a complete vector-to-mitigation matrix.\n- Unmitigated vectors are identified with recommended remediation scope, measured by a documented remediation list (which may be empty).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Validates the P0 HLRs \"Artifact requests ... are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" and \"Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed\", and the Security NFR (all artifact access in workspace-aware mode validated through the existing auth plugin infrastructure; failure to validate results in access denial).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75234,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88698f900cb0bd7f","agent_id":"a1b8946567b2496a2","session_id":"1a623240-3c0b-475a-b0a3-8024f6d16813","uuid":"da690c78-7e53-40ba-9bce-6d9192deba02","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:15:21.201Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
