{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab80cc8113a30f01","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E007\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E008\n14\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n15\tgate_failure_impact:\n16\t  action: rewrite\n17\t  fallback_approach: If artifact request paths do not carry sufficient information\n18\t    to resolve workspace membership, the server's workspace-validation approach is\n19\t    rewritten per the path-resolvability findings (e.g. alternative resolution mechanism\n20\t    or denial of unresolvable paths); remediation items from the fail-mode and security\n21\t    analyses are incorporated.\n22\tai_signals:\n23\t  change_specificity: 0\n24\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n25\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n26\t  existing_foundation: 1\n27\t  open_questions: -1\n28\t  external_dependency: 1\n29\t  human_process_gates: 0\n30\t  repo_access: 1\n31\t  architecture_claims: 1\n32\timplementation_type: null\n33\tinvestigation_signals: null\n34\tai_implementability: High\n35\tai_implementability_score: 5\n36\tjira_key: null\n37\tbranch: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n40\t\n41\t## Description\n42\t\n43\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests that fail workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403. The mode is fail-closed: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied rather than served without validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer direction). Includes audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs.\n44\t\n45\tThis epic is gated by the investigations resolving artifact path resolvability, failure modes, and security bypass vectors, and builds on the plugin workspace-resolution capability for the artifacts-only context (the server's validation path builds against the plugin's resolution logic, so the plugin work lands first).\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- Server initialization: allow the `--artifacts-only` + `--enable-workspaces` combination; require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` when both are set (fail to start with a clear error if missing); initialize only the workspace provider; keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints disabled.\n50\t- Artifact request handling: resolve each artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store; delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny failing requests with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n51\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active and the workspace store is unreachable, deny artifact requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable; requests with invalid or missing workspace context are denied, not served.\n52\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` preserves current behavior (serving without workspace validation); omitting either flag preserves current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n53\t- Audit logging (P2): workspace access denials in artifact serving generate audit entries through server logs.\n54\t- Performance: measure per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions if the measured overhead exceeds the acceptable threshold agreed with PM/Engineering, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path.\n55\t- Scope the initial implementation to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) where paths follow the well-defined experiment-scoped structure; document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n56\t\n57\t## Acceptance Criteria\n58\t\n59\t- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served, measured by HTTP 200 response with correct artifact content.\n60\t- Given workspace-aware serving enabled and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 403 response with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n61\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation, measured by HTTP 200 response regardless of workspace membership.\n62\t- Given workspace-aware mode active and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 503 response indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n63\t- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` started without `--workspace-store-uri`, then the server fails to start with a clear error.\n64\t- Given workspace-aware mode, non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled and tracking/model registry stores remain uninitialized, measured by server startup behavior and endpoint availability.\n65\t- Given a workspace access denial in artifact serving, an audit log entry is produced through server logs.\n66\t- Per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and documented; short-TTL caching is implemented if the overhead exceeds the agreed threshold.\n67\t- Limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- P0: `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving.\n72\t- P0: Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403.\n73\t- P0: Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied.\n74\t- P0: Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes.\n75\t- P2: Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled here — it shares the denial-handling code path with the P0 workspace-validation work).\n76\t\n77\t## Risks\n78\t\n79\t- Artifact path resolution complexity: scoped to the S3 artifact store; limitations documented for non-standard artifact roots.\n80\t- Performance impact of per-request workspace resolution: measurement plus conditional short-TTL caching per the tracking server's existing auth-path caching patterns.\n81\t- Failure modes and bypass vectors: confirmed by the fail-mode and security analyses that gate this epic; any remediation they identify is incorporated.\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab99201f8d919049","agent_id":"adde90d1badb39ce7","session_id":"1a623240-3c0b-475a-b0a3-8024f6d16813","uuid":"8c9e3c79-09cf-445c-98c9-c61d24487c1d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:20:22.125Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab80cc8113a30f01","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
