is unverified.\n34\terror: null\n35\t---\n36\t## Review Summary\n37\t\n38\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n39\tRecommendation: accept\n40\t\n41\tFull decomposition (triage absent), 8 main-plan epics plus 2 conditional branch epics. The decomposition is structurally sound: DAG edges are fully consistent between the summary diagram and epic frontmatter, all HLRs are traced, both bounded routing outcomes have branch epics, Staff Engineer overrides (no new flag, split deployment, no SSAR prescription) are correctly applied, and Rule 19 safety-critical investigations (fail-mode + security) both block the main implementation. One signal-scoring inconsistency (E001) costs a point on Criterion 5; the remaining findings are minor.\n42\t\n43\t## Criterion Details\n44\t\n45\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n46\tTraceability matrix present and complete. All four P0 HLRs map to E004 (with E005/E002/E007/E008 as enabling/gating epics); the three P1 HLRs map to E006, E001/E005, and E003 plus the conditional branch epics. The P2 audit-logging HLR is bundled into E004 with explicit justification (shares the 403-denial code path with the P0 workspace-validation work — tightly coupled, not independently deferrable), which matches the Step 4 bundling rule; this is not priority collapse. Priority inheritance is correct: E001/E005/E007/E008 are P0 because they transitively enable/block the P0 server work; E003 and E006 remain P1 gating only P1 HLRs; no prerequisite epic has lower priority than work it enables. Multi-epic output is expected (triage absent), so no triage contradiction.\n47\t\n48\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n49\tEvery main-plan diagram edge (E001→E005, E002→E004, E005→E004, E007→E004, E008→E004, E003→E006, E008→E006) has a matching entry in the target epic's `dependencies`, and vice versa — no orphaned frontmatter dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics, no cycles. Arrow direction follows the `graph TD` dependency→dependent convention. Branch epics (BRANCH-A-E009, BRANCH-B-E009) both set `branch` and `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8009-E003` with `gated_by` present in `dependencies`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies. No konflux-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable; the decomposer correctly resisted a `konflux/general`-style edge between E004 and E006 — the deployed image's flag support is handled as a configuration-reference AC, exactly per Rule 13. The E005→E004 edge is a legitimate Rule 12 library→consumer edge (the server's validation path builds against the plugin's resolution API). The five investigations run in parallel (Rule 14) and E004 ∥ E006 parallelism is preserved. Critical path length 3 (E001→E005→E004) is reasonable for an M-size DAG with no new components. No data/artifact dependency discovered in epic content is missing from the DAG: branch epics correctly depend on E006 (they configure routing toward the instance it deploys), and end-to-end routing validation is an AC gate, not an edge.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n52\tAll epics are owned by the MLflow Team per the strategy's Impacted Teams table, and implementation work is correctly split by repo/architectural sub-system: MLflow server (E004), mlflow-kubernetes-plugins (E005), MLflow operator (E006, branch A), with routing delegated to the branch epics. Component names all use the canonical `MLflow` entry from `.context/rhai-components.txt` (no separate MLflow Operator/plugin entries exist); the non-obvious mappings are logged in the summary. No scope duplication: the two branch epics are mutually exclusive outcomes of the same investigation, and E007 (failure-mode matrix) vs E008 (bypass-vector threat analysis) produce distinct deliverables mandated by Rule 19. No work-product type mixing — documentation is limited to incidental limitation notes inside E004. Minor observation: E004 is the densest epic (P0 validation + fail-closed + backwards compatibility + P2 audit logging + latency measurement/conditional caching), but the strategy estimates the server-side scope at 1-2 sprints and the P2/perf items share the denial code path, so it stays within the ~2-week boundary.\n53\t\n54\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n55\tAll five Investigations genuinely resolve unknowns that change downstream scope or existence (plugin coupling → verify-vs-refactor; path resolvability → validation approach; routing mechanism → which branch epic exists; failure modes and bypass vectors → possible remediation), and each has downstream dependents. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Every epic with `gated_by` has a complete `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E004→E002, E005→E001, E006→E003, both branches→E003). Minor observation: E004 also depends on Investigations E007/E008 and E006 on E008, whose `add_remediation` gate semantics are recorded in the `gate_failure_impact` prose and the summary's DAG justification rather than in `gated_by` — acceptable given `gated_by` is single-valued and Rule 19 mandates two blocking investigations on the main implementation, but consumers that detect gates solely via `gated_by` will not see the fail-mode/security gates.\n56\t\n57\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n58\tSignal sets are type-correct everywhere (Investigations carry `investigation_signals`, Implementations carry `ai_signals`; no epic mixes them), all ten `-ai-signals.md` rationale files exist, and nearly all values are well justified — e.g., E005's `repo_access: 0` / `architecture_claims: 0` for the plugin lacking an architecture-context file, branch B's `pattern_precedent: 0` / `adapter_pattern: 0` for the novel delegation pattern, and E004's `open_questions: -1` given the rewrite gate. One arguable value: E001 scores `cluster_hardware_dependence: 0` (\"no peripheral question requires a live cluster\") while its own `local_runnability: 0` rationale states \"runtime confirmation needs a cluster,\" and its AC requires runtime verification evidence when the plugin is not coupled. Per the rubric, a peripheral question needing a live cluster scores -1 — E003/E007/E008 score -1 on equivalent peripheral cluster needs, so E001's 0 is internally inconsistent with its own rationale. E008's `human_judgment_required: -1` (peripheral Security/PM sign-off on residual risk) is borderline but defensible under the rubric.\n59\t\n60\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n61\tEvery epic has testable, Given/When/Then-style ACs with \"measured by\" clauses, directly derived from the strategy's six acceptance criteria (AC 1-4 → E004; AC 5-6 → E006) plus HLR-derived ACs (fail-to-start on missing `--workspace-store-uri`, non-artifact endpoints disabled, operator validation rejection, conditional-CRD behavior). Rule-mandated ACs: no konflux chain exists (Rule 25 N/A), no docs-authoring epic exists (Rule 24 N/A), and the strategy adds a capability rather than replacing one (Rule 23 N/A) — backwards-compatibility ACs are present regardless. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; dependencies are described in plain terms (\"the routing investigation\", \"the operator topology work\").\n62\t\n63\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n64\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: the six proposed acceptance criteria, all NFRs (Security → E004/E008, Backwards Compatibility → E004, Performance → E004 measurement + conditional caching AC), all four risks (path resolution → E002/E004 with S3 scoping, plugin coupling → E001/E005, split-topology complexity → E006 + operator validation, per-request latency → E004), and all four open questions are explicitly handled (latency → E004 AC, routing → E003, plugin coupling → E001, audit channel → implemented per HLR with scope-growth warning). Out-of-scope items are respected. The conditional branches cover both bounded routing outcomes. Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes), so the absence of a docs-authoring epic is correct. Staff Engineer overrides are applied across all epics (no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag anywhere; E006 is a true split deployment; no SSAR prescriptions). Minor observation: mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no architecture-context file, so it is not \"in platform\" by the strict architecture-context test and no onboarding chain was generated — the decomposer's justification (strategy marks it internal/exists, mlflow.md documents it shipping in the MLflow image, flagged as Health Warning 4 for human verification of repo accessibility) is reasonable and transparently recorded.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a475d07b6e67a867","agent_id":"a2ce6aa8ea2244490","session_id":"1a623240-3c0b-475a-b0a3-8024f6d16813","uuid":"8aaffa86-eb35-4141-ad50-98b205d71ba1","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:28:34.385Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-96410bdc9f345a1f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
