{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a48cc5ab7d6dbc9b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n13\t**Status**: Full decomposition (not triaged below-threshold or docs-only)\n14\t\n15\t## Triage Rationale\n16\t\n17\tThe strategy affects three active components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins), is estimated M-L (4-7 sprints), and spans architecturally distinct work streams (Python server flag composition, plugin workspace resolution, Go operator split-deployment topology with Helm chart templates). Neither Step 0 check applies.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace-resolution initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path resolvability for workspace validation | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate split-topology artifact traffic routing mechanism | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Fail-mode analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | Security bypass analysis of workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n31\t\n32\t### Conditional branch epics (attached to E003, not standalone Jira issues)\n33\t\n34\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Branch |\n35\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|--------|\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009 | Gateway-level artifact traffic routing to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | A |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009 | Tracking-server artifact delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | B |\n38\t\n39\t## Dependency DAG (main plan)\n40\t\n41\t```mermaid\n42\tgraph TD\n43\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8009-E001] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005]\n44\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8009-E002] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n45\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-8009-E005] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n46\t    E007[RHAISTRAT-8009-E007] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n47\t    E008[RHAISTRAT-8009-E008] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8009-E004]\n48\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003] --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006]\n49\t    E008[RHAISTRAT-8009-E008] --> E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006]\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\t### Conditional branches from E003\n53\t\n54\t```mermaid\n55\tgraph TD\n56\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8009-E003] --> BA[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009]\n57\t    E003 --> BB[RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009]\n58\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-8009-E006] --> BA\n59\t    E006 --> BB\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tOnly one branch is realized, depending on the E003 outcome. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n63\t\n64\t## DAG Justification\n65\t\n66\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n67\t|------|------|-----------|\n68\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Open question \"Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" determines whether the plugin work is verification-only or requires refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — scope/approach gate (`rewrite`). |\n69\t| E001 → E005 (existence) | Rule 3 | The unknown is strategy-flagged (open question, risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\", assumption \"workspace store accessible without initializing tracking stores\") and its answer changes what the plugin epic does. |\n70\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 | Assumption \"artifact request paths include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged needs-validation; if paths do not resolve, the server's workspace-validation approach rewrites (`rewrite` gate). |\n71\t| E005 → E004 | Rule 12 (library → consumer serial) | The MLflow server's artifacts-only workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution (loaded via `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / WorkspaceStoreRegistry). If the plugin's initialization path is refactored, the server-side wiring consumes that change. |\n72\t| E007 → E004 | Rule 19 (safety-critical: fail-mode Investigation) | Workspace-aware artifact serving is a multi-tenant access-control feature (tenant isolation, fail-closed authorization). The fail-mode analysis enumerates failure modes and confirms fail-closed coverage; may reveal modes requiring additional remediation (`add_remediation` gate). |\n73\t| E008 → E004 | Rule 19 (safety-critical: security Investigation) | Bypass-vector analysis (crafted paths, direct instance access) gates the main server implementation; may reveal remediation work (`add_remediation` gate). |\n74\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing?\" — answer \"affects HTTPRoute design\", so the operator topology epic is gated (`rewrite`). |\n75\t| E008 → E006 | Rule 19 | Risk \"Split-deployment operational complexity\" states misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation; the security analysis covers operator misconfiguration vectors and gates the topology implementation (`add_remediation` gate). |\n76\t| E003 → branch epics | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes → conditional branches) | Two bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing suffices; (B) tracking-server delegation required. Each outcome selects a different branch epic (`remove` gate on the non-selected branch). |\n77\t| E006 → branch epics | Rule 12/13 | Both routing branches configure traffic toward the dedicated artifacts-only instance; they consume the deployed instance produced by the operator topology epic. |\n78\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E007 ∥ E008 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Independent investigations resolving distinct unknowns (plugin init coupling, path resolvability, routing mechanism, failure modes, bypass vectors); no shared deliverable — not combined. |\n79\t| E004 ∥ E006 (no edge) | Rule 14 + Rule 13 clarification | Different repos (mlflow vs mlflow-operator); the operator renders Helm templates referencing server flags — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. Verified by AC (\"deployed image supports the combined flags\"), never a DAG edge. |\n80\t| Branch epics ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rules 14/15 | Routing configuration does not build against the server's workspace-validation code (different repos / different areas in the same repo); end-to-end routing validation is an AC gate, not a DAG dependency. |\n81\t| No docs-authoring epic | Step 5 rule 11 boundary | Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes) and no substantive content authoring is in scope; incidental doc touch-ups (documenting limitations for non-standard artifact roots) stay inside the originating epics as scope/AC. |\n82\t| No onboarding chain | Rule 20 N/A | MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files and existing Konflux builds; mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is an existing internal component (see Health Warnings). |\n83\t\n84\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n85\t\n86\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n87\t|-----|----------|---------|\n88\t| `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 |\n89\t| Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004, E005 (enables), E002 (validates approach), E008 (validates no bypass) |\n90\t| Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004, E007 (validates failure-mode coverage) |\n91\t| Single-tenant / no-workspaces deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 |\n92\t| Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) | P1 | E006 |\n93\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance | P1 | E003 (selects mechanism), RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009 or RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional) |\n94\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context | P1 | E001 (resolves coupling unknown), E005 |\n95\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E004 (bundled — shares the denial-handling code path with P0 workspace validation; not independently deferrable) |\n96\t\n97\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. P2 audit logging is bundled into E004 because it shares the 403-denial implementation surface with the P0 workspace-validation work (tightly coupled — splitting would duplicate the denial path).\n98\t\n99\t## Priority Notes\n100\t\n101\t- E005 and E001 carry P0 despite their directly-mapped HLR being P1: priority inheritance — the plugin's artifacts-only workspace resolution transitively enables the P0 workspace-validation HLR via E004.\n102\t- E007 and E008 are P0: they block E004, which implements all P0 HLRs.\n103\t- E003 and E006 are P1: they gate only P1 HLRs (operator topology and routing).\n104\t\n105\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n106\t\n107\tThe Staff Engineer Input section is non-empty and takes precedence; the AI-generated technical approach was corrected accordingly:\n108\t\n109\t1. **No new flag** — the proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed. All epic scope uses the combined-flags approach (`--artifacts-only` + `--enable-workspaces` + required `--workspace-store-uri`). No epic introduces a new server flag.\n110\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — E006 deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance (separate Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute) and the routing work is decomposed as conditional epics on the routing-mechanism investigation; no epic treats this as a single CR field toggle.\n111\t3. **SSAR detail over-specified** — epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview/pseudo-resource prescriptions in any epic scope). The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are explicitly unchanged.\n112\t\n113\t## Component Name Mappings\n114\t\n115\tCanonical component names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n116\t\n117\t- **MLflow Operator** → `MLflow` (no operator-specific canonical component exists; MLflow is the closest parent-level component). Applied to E003, E006, RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009.\n118\t- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** → `MLflow` (closest parent-level component; the plugin ships inside the MLflow deployment per mlflow.md architecture context). Applied to E001, E005.\n119\t- **MLflow** → `MLflow` (exact match). Applied to E002, E004, E007, E008, RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009.\n120\t\n121\t## Investigation Summary\n122\t\n123\t| Epic | Unknown resolved | Why it changes downstream structure/scope | Downstream gate |\n124\t|------|------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------|\n125\t| E001 | Plugin workspace-resolution initialization coupling | Determines verify-only vs refactor of the plugin | E005 (rewrite) |\n126\t| E002 | Artifact path → experiment/workspace resolvability | Determines the server's workspace-validation approach | E004 (rewrite) |\n127\t| E003 | Routing mechanism (gateway-level vs tracking-server delegation) | Determines which routing implementation exists and HTTPRoute design | E006 (rewrite); branch A/B epics (remove) |\n128\t| E007 | Failure-mode surface of workspace-aware serving | May reveal modes requiring remediation beyond specified fail-closed design | E004 (add_remediation) |\n129\t| E008 | Validation bypass vectors in split topology | May reveal unmitigated vectors requiring additional work in server/operator | E004, E006 (add_remediation) |\n130\t\n131\tOpen questions NOT promoted to Investigations (with handling):\n132\t- **Latency overhead / caching at launch** (PM/Engineering) — does not change which epics exist; captured as measurement + conditional short-TTL caching AC on E004. See Health Warnings.\n133\t- **Audit telemetry channel** (PM/Security) — HLR already specifies server logs; implemented as specified in E004, with scope-growth risk flagged in Health Warnings.\n134\t- Assumption \"flags composable without breaking init sequence\" — the work itself (E004); failure = fix within the epic.\n135\t- Assumption \"Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes\" — the work itself (E006).\n136\t- Assumption \"single-tenant deployments do not set --enable-workspaces\" — backwards-compatibility AC on E004 (strategy HLR P0-4 requires current behavior preserved regardless).\n137\t\n138\t## Conditional Branches\n139\t\n140\tE003 (routing mechanism investigation) has two bounded outcomes that change downstream structure:\n141\t\n142\t- **Branch A — gateway-level routing suffices**: HTTPRoute path-based rules route artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance; no tracking-server change. Realized by `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009` (MLflow Operator work).\n143\t- **Branch B — tracking-server delegation required**: the main tracking server needs a configuration/code change to delegate artifact operations. Realized by `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009` (MLflow server work).\n144\t\n145\tBoth branch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8009-E003` (also in `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n146\t\n147\t## Health Warnings\n148\t\n149\t1. **Priority observation (non-blocking)**: the P0 HLRs (server-side workspace-aware serving) only deliver user-facing value in multi-tenant deployments once the P1 operator topology (E006) and routing land. The P1 work sits on the value-realization path of the P0 scope — consider whether the operator epic should be pulled forward in planning.\n150\t2. **Performance NFR has no latency target**: the strategy's Performance NFR defers to an open PM/Engineering question. Caching-at-launch vs deferred is undetermined; handled as a measurement + conditional-caching AC on E004. May spawn follow-up work.\n151\t3. **Audit channel scope risk (P2)**: HLR-8 specifies server logs, but open question 4 (PM/Security) may expand the channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log). Implemented per HLR; scope growth would be a follow-up.\n152\t4. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins not in architecture context**: no architecture context file exists for it, but the strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\" and mlflow.md documents it shipping in the MLflow image (v1.5.0, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`). Treated as an existing internal component — no repo-onboarding/konflux-onboarding chain generated. Flagged for human verification that the team's plugin repo is accessible for modification.\n153\t5. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" — outstanding human gates at the strategy level that may affect epic sequencing.\n154\t6. **Scope note (S3-only path resolution)**: per the strategy's risk mitigation, the initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store with the standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout; limitations for non-standard artifact roots must be documented (AC on E004).\n155\t\n156\t## Tiered Delivery\n157\t\n158\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API conditional CRD discovery, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar); no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is required. The P0 (server capability) → P1 (operator topology) ordering reflects HLR priorities, not dependency tiering.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-875a96a172fb2b4e","agent_id":"a83d4d905b3d9d722","session_id":"1a623240-3c0b-475a-b0a3-8024f6d16813","uuid":"33d16eae-2f86-4bb3-ade7-f6d53ba235df","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:30:55.735Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a48cc5ab7d6dbc9b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
